a procedural/clinical network organized around safe and high-quality image acquisition and its downstream uses [Data: Relationships (6438, 13856, 8671, 8676, 12301)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Patient motion control is a recurring operational need addressed through supports and immobilization\",\"explanation\":\"The community highlights that maintaining stillness is important for image quality, with pillows and immobilization devices used to reduce movement. The patient may be positioned with PILLOWS to help keep still during the scan, and STRAPS OR IMMOBILIZATION DEVICES may be used to prevent movement during MRI [Data: Relationships (6430, 8653); Entities (3276, 4190)].\\n\\nMRI-specific positioning aids are also represented as PILLOWS OR BOLSTERS, explicitly tied to MRI SCAN for positioning and helping patients remain still, reinforcing the emphasis on minimizing motion artifacts as a practical constraint of MRI acquisition [Data: Relationships (6934); Entities (3449)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Breath-holding (10–20 seconds) is a standardized instruction tied to image collection timing\",\"explanation\":\"Breath-holding appears as both an instruction and a named action (HOLDING BREATH / BREATH-HOLDING / BREATH HOLDING), consistently described as a brief 10–20 second pause while images are collected to reduce motion from breathing and improve image quality [Data: Entities (1559, 3748, 5238)].\\n\\nThe relationships connect this requirement both at the patient-instruction level (PATIENT → HOLDING BREATH) and as a procedural feature of MRI SCAN (MRI SCAN → BREATH-HOLDING and MRI SCAN → BREATH HOLDING), indicating that breath-hold instructions are a routine operational component during certain MRI sequences [Data: Relationships (2482, 7731, 11607)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The IMAGING ROOM introduces a strong safety and compliance dimension due to magnetic hazards\",\"explanation\":\"The IMAGING ROOM is defined as a controlled scanning area for MRI with strict safety restrictions, specifically prohibiting bringing metal objects inside due to the powerful magnets and risks of hazards or scan interference [Data: Entities (3250)].\\n\\nThis is directly reinforced by the relationship stating MRI SCAN is performed in the IMAGING ROOM and that metal objects should not be brought in during the scan to ensure safety and prevent interference with equipment, positioning safety compliance as a key operational dependency of MRI delivery [Data: Relationships (6438)].\"},{\"summary\":\"MRI can be limited by patient tolerance for small enclosed spaces, affecting planning and communication\",\"explanation\":\"SMALL ENCLOSED SPACES are explicitly represented as a patient concern (claustrophobia-related) relevant to undergoing MRI, framing a barrier that can influence whether and how imaging is performed [Data: Entities (6080)].\\n\\nThe network links this directly to MRI SCAN via guidance that patients should tell their doctor if they have issues with small enclosed spaces when planning MRI, implying the need for pre-scan screening and accommodation planning as part of workflow readiness [Data: Relationships (13856)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Clinical downstream uses: MRI supports diagnosis/treatment pathways and post-therapy assessment\",\"explanation\":\"DIAGNOSIS AND TREATMENT is described as the phase where lymphoma diagnosis and treatment planning/delivery occur, with MRI used early when symptoms suggest a brain tumor to identify suspicious findings and after treatment to evaluate response; it also notes biopsy for definitive diagnosis when imaging suggests lymphoma [Data: Entities (1358)].\\n\\nThis is captured in the relationship connecting MRI SCAN to DIAGNOSIS AND TREATMENT, emphasizing MRI’s role both in initial evaluation and in assessing response after treatment, making MRI a repeated touchpoint in longitudinal care management rather than a one-time test [Data: Relationships (8676)].\"},{\"summary\":\"MRI contributes to surgery planning by providing soft-tissue detail and tumor feature visualization\",\"explanation\":\"SURGERY PLANNING is explicitly tied to MRI’s ability to show soft tissues and tumor-related details that help guide surgical planning, indicating a decision-support role beyond detection and staging [Data: Entities (5516)].\\n\\nThe relationship MRI SCAN → SURGERY PLANNING states that MRI scans show soft tissues and details helpful for planning surgery, reinforcing MRI as an enabling technology for preoperative assessment and planning workflows [Data: Relationships (12301)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Attribution and educational documentation: a named physician is credited as an MRI example image source\",\"explanation\":\"A specific individual, Stephanie E. Weiss, MD, FASTRO, is included as a credited source of an illustrative MRI image demonstrating how primary CNS lymphoma may appear on brain imaging, indicating the community includes not only operational steps but also reference/educational materials tied to MRI interpretation [Data: Entities (4173)].\\n\\nThis attribution is formalized in the relationship linking her to MRI SCAN as the source of an example image illustrating primary CNS lymphoma appearance, suggesting a documentation lineage element (source credit) within the broader MRI information ecosystem [Data: Relationships (8661)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Workflow mechanics: the scanning table is a defined component of the MRI patient pathway\",\"explanation\":\"The SCANNING TABLE is identified as the table the patient lies on that slides into the MRI machine, representing a concrete procedural element in the imaging workflow [Data: Entities (4176)].\\n\\nThe relationship MRI SCAN → SCANNING TABLE specifies that during MRI the patient lies on a table that slides into the scanning machine, situating patient positioning and movement into the scanner as a standard mechanical step in the overall procedure [Data: Relationships (8671)].\"}],\"rating\":3.2,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community reflects a routine bu